# SYNOPSIS

```usage
plumber
```

# DESCRIPTION

The plumber routes plumb messages (plumb(6)) between programs by the rules the user writes (07 §7): a program writes a message, and the plumber decides, by the rules, which program it is for.
It is 9front's plumber on `lib/vx-plumb`, its files, ports and rule language kept as 9front has them.
svcd(8) starts it from `boot/svc/plumber.ndb`, as the shell's user and in the shell's tree, and posts it as `/srv/plumb`; the shell and the terminal have it at `/mnt/plumb`, and so does the plumber itself, for the programs its rules start: it mounts its own post there once it serves, and until then holds writes to `send` and `rules`.

Its tree:

: `send`
  A message written here is matched against the rules (plumb(6)) and sent from the port of the first ruleset that applies, or, with no port, its `start` command run.
  A message may come in several writes.
  One no ruleset applies to goes to the port its `dst` names, unchanged; with none, the write fails.
: `rules`
  Read, the rules as they stand: the variables, the ports, then each ruleset, as 9front lists them.
  Written, rulesets are added, each as its blank line comes and the rest at the close; an open with `OTRUNC` clears the rules first.
  A write with an error in it fails, and the plumber says the error on its console.
  One program may have it open for writing at a time.
: <port>
  A file for each port the rules have named with `plumb to`, read only.
  Each open of a port reads its own copy of every message sent from it after it was opened, one message a read, in parts if a read is too small; a read with no message to give waits for one.
  A port stays when the rules no longer name it.

A message for a port no program has open runs the ruleset's `start` or `client` command, if it has one: a `start` throws the message away, and a `client` holds it for the next open of the port, which the command is expected to make.
A message for such a port whose ruleset has neither fails.
A command is run in the plumber's namespace, its first word a path when it holds a `/`, else a name in `/bin`; it has no standard input and the plumber's standard output and error.

The rules are read when the plumber starts, from `$home/lib/plumbing` on an installed system (`/home/lib/plumbing` in its tree), else from `/lib/plumb/basic`.
A name under `/mnt/plumb` is never looked up, by `isfile`, `isdir` or `include`: the plumber would be asking itself while it serves.

# EXAMPLES

A program that reads the editor's port, and a click sent to it:

```rc
% cat /mnt/plumb/edit &
% plumb -w /tmp 'notes.txt:3'
plumb
edit
/tmp
text
addr=3
14
/tmp/notes.txt
```

A rule added for a while:

```rc
% echo 'type is text
data matches ''hello''
plumb start /bin/echo plumbed $0
' >> /mnt/plumb/rules
% plumb hello
plumbed hello
```

# FILES

: `/lib/plumb/basic`
  The system's rules.
: `/lib/plumb/fileaddr`
  `$addr`, a file's address, which `basic` includes.
: `$home/lib/plumbing`
  The user's rules, read instead.

# SEE ALSO

plumb(1), plumb(6), svcd(8)

# DIAGNOSTICS

At its start the plumber says `plumber: serving /srv/plumb, rules from` the file and how many ports it has, and the first error in the rules, if any, by file and line.
`plumber: rules:` and the error, at a write to `rules` that has one; `plumber: cannot start` and the command, when a rule's command does not start.

# BUGS

Replies carry a status, not 9front's error string: a message nothing takes is `not found`, a malformed one `invalid`, a second writer of `rules` `bad state`.
Attaching is not checked against the plumber's user, as 9front's is.
A port's file is read only by programs that opened it before a message came; 9front's is the same.
